2 CONT
2 CONT == Evidence from the National Records of Scotland ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''4 November 1513: Perth. Vicecomes respondebit pro 2 d. argenti per duplicationem albefirme terrarum baronie de  Fyngask, regi debitam per sasinam datam Alexandro Dundas de eisdem 4 Novemb.  a.r.l. [https://archive.org/details/e
2 CONC xchequerrollsof14scot/page/522/mode/1up RSRS: p. 522]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == Printed Evidence ==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''[https://archive.org/details/rotuliscaccariir14grea/page/n8/mode/1up Rotuli Scaccarii Regum Scotorum. The Exchequer Rolls of Scotland. Vol. XIV. 1513-1522. Edited by A. J. G. Mackay, MA. Oxon., LL.D. Edin., Honorary Fellow of K
2 CONC ing's College, London; Sheriff of Perth and Kinross. (H. M. General Register House, Edinburgh, 1893), 836 pp. including index and corrigenda]'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Pinkie_Cleugh Battle of Pinkie Cleugh] ==
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Pinkie Cleugh, sometimes known as the Battle of Pinkie, took place on 10 September 1547 on the banks of the River Esk near Musselburgh, Scotland. The last pitched battle between Scottish and English armies, it was pa
2 CONC rt of the conflict known as the Rough Wooing, and is considered to be the first modern battle in the British Isles. It was a catastrophic defeat for Scotland, where it became known as Black Saturday.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Pinkie_Cleugh
